  "verbalization": "Cefuroxime, a broad-spectrum antibiotic, is implicated in the treatment of infections caused by Escherichia coli through a series of interconnected indications. The evidence suggests that Cefuroxime may exert its antibacterial effect by targeting bacterial cell walls, as indicated by its use in treating rhinoscleroma, streptococcal meningitis, and acute gonococcal endometritis, all of which are associated with Escherichia coli infections. The shared pathway involves the use of related antibiotics, such as aztreonam, ceftizoxime, and cefoxitin, which are also known to inhibit bacterial growth by disrupting cell wall synthesis. The final scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in the relationship, suggesting that Cefuroxime may act through a mechanism involving cell wall inhibition, potentially in a manner similar to other cephalosporins. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Cefuroxime and Escherichia coli infection, though further studies are needed to confirm the exact mode of action and to assess its efficacy in treating this specific bacterial infection."
